y thought 24*80 is 24*8*10=192*10=1920, then 24*8=192, then multiplied those two results? 1920*192 would be way too big. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 24*88 as (20+4)*(80+8) and messed up one term. Let's see:\n\n20*80=1600,\n\n20*8=160,\n\n4*80=320,\n\n4*8=32.\n\nAdding those: 1600+160=1760; 320+32=352. Then 1760+352=2112. So if they miscalculated one of these terms, like 4*80=320 but maybe they thought it was 400, then 1760+ (400+32)=1760+432=2192, which still isn't 2612. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they thought 88 is 90-2, so 24*(90-2)=24*90 -24*2. If they miscalculated 24*90 as 24*9*10=216*10=2160. Then subtract 24*2=48, so 2160-48=2112. So that's correct. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 24*88 as 24* (8*11)=24*8*11=192*11. Then 192*10=1920 +192=2112. \n\nHmm. I'm trying to figure out why the user thought the answer was 2612, but all my calculations lead to 2112. Perhaps they added 192 + 1920 as 2112 but then thought they were supposed to add another number? Like maybe they considered the hundreds place? Wait, 88 is only two digits, so there is no hundreds place. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they misread the numbers. The question says 24 and 88, but maybe they thought it was 24 and 113 or something else? If it was 24*113, then 24*100=2400, 24*10=240, 24*3=72; total 2400+240=2640+72=2712. Still not 2612. \n\nAlternatively, maybe they multiplied 24*88 as 24*(80+8) and then added 24*80=1920 and 24*8=192, but added 1920+192 as 1920+192=2112. So unless they added 1920 + 600, which would be 2520, but where would the 600 come from?\n\nAlternatively, perhaps they thought the second partial product was 24*800 instead of 24*80, but that would be 19200. Then adding 192 +19200 = 19392. Not 2612.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they did 24*88 as (24*10) + (24*78). 24*10=240, 24*78= 24*(70+8)= 1680 + 192= 1872. Then 240+1872=2112. Still same.\n\nHmm. Alternatively, maybe they added 192 and 1920 as 192+1920 = 2112, but thought they needed to multiply the two partial products? 192 *1920, 