 Or is it 69 × 8 (the digit) and then multiplied by 10? Let me check the example again.\n\nIn the example, number2 was 153. The tens place digit was 5, so they multiplied by 50. So they treated the tens digit as 50. So for 80\'s tens digit, which is 8, that\'s 80. So step 2 is 69 × 80. Wait, but that\'s the same as the entire number? Wait no, the original problem is 69 × 80. Wait, that can\'t be right. Wait, maybe I\'m misunderstanding the approach here. Let me think again.\n\nWait, actually, when multiplying by each digit\'s place, like the standard algorithm, you multiply the first number by each digit of the second, shifted appropriately. So for 69 × 80, since 80 is two digits, you first multiply 69 by 0 (the ones place), then 69 by 8 (the tens place) but shifted one position to the left (equivalent to multiplying by 80). .\n\nSo in the example, they did 27×3 (ones place), then 27×50 (tens place: 5×10), then 27×100 (hundreds place). So following that, for 80, the steps would be:.\n\n1. 69 × 0 (ones place).\n2. 69 × 80 (tens place).\n\nThen sum those two results. .\n\nCalculating step 1: 69 × 0 = 0.\n\nStep 2: 69 × 80. Let me compute that</think>\nTherefore, the answer is ']]
