ultiplied by 5. 30 times 5 is straightforward. 3 times 5 is 15, so 30 times 5 is 150. Got that.\n\nSecond step: 30 multiplied by 30. Wait a second, 30 times 30. Hmm, 3 times 3 is 9, and then adding the zeros: 30 has one zero, 30 has another, so total two zeros, making it 900? Wait, but hold on, actually, 30 times 30 is 900? Let me check that again. Alternatively, 30 times 30 can be thought of as (3 x 10) x (3 x 10) = 9 x 100 = 900. Yes, that's correct.\n\nWait, but maybe another way: 30 times 30 is the same as 30 multiplied by 3 tens, so 30 x 30 = 30 x (3 x 10) = (30 x 3) x 10 = 90 x 10 = 900. Yep, that checks out.\n\nNow, adding the two results: 150 (from the ones place) and 900 (from the tens place)</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
