inal template example had three steps for a three-digit number. Here, 60 is a two-digit number, so the reasoning would have two steps. Let me outline it properly:\n\n1. Multiply 76 by the ones place digit (0) of 60, resulting in 0.\n2. Multiply 76 by the tens place digit (6) which is actually 60, so 76*60\'s tens place contribution is 76*6*10 = 4560. Wait, maybe breaking it down as per place value:\n\nAlternatively, in the template example, when they had 153 (three digits), they broke it into three steps. So for 60, which is two digits, the steps would be:\n\n1. Multiply 76 by 0 (ones place)\n2. Multiply 76 by 60 (the tens place, but since it\'s 6 in the tens place, it\'s 6*10, so 76*6*10). \n\nWait, perhaps more precisely:\n\nBreaking down 60 into 6*10, but following the place value steps:\n\nStep 1: Multiply 76 by 0 (the ones digit of 60) → 0\nStep 2: Multiply 76 by 6 (the tens digit, but since it\'s in the tens place, it\'s actually 60, so the result is 76*6*10 = 4560)\n\nThen sum the two steps: 0 + 4560 = 4560. \n\nTherefore, the answer is 4560. The user\'s initial thought of 4960 is incorrect, likely due to an arithmetic mistake in multiplying 76 by 6. So I need to present this step-by-step as per the template, showing the correct calculation.\n</think>', '']
