s place, since 5 is in the tens place, so 5*10).\n3. Then the hundreds place 100 (1*100), so 27 * 100.\n\nThen sum all those. So applying that to 11 * 60:.\n\n60 is 6 in tens and 0 in ones, so three digits? Wait no, 60 is two digits. So the places are ones and tens. So step 1: multiply by ones digit (0), step 2: multiply by tens digit (6) but as 60. Wait, actually, maybe the way to do it is:.\n\nFirst, multiply 11 by the ones digit (0) giving 0. Then multiply 11 by the tens digit (6) but since it's the tens place, you have to multiply by 6*10, so 60. Wait, but that's the same as the original problem. That can't be right. Maybe I need to separate the digits properly.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the standard algorithm is to do:.\n\n11 × 60 can be thought of as 11 × 6 × 10. Since 60 is 6×10</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
