hat? Well, 43 times 50 is the same as 43 times 5 times 10. Let's see. 43*5 is 215, then times 10 is 2150. Alternatively, 43*50 can be thought of as 43*(5*10) = (43*5)*10. Either way, the result should be 2150. .\n\nWait, but maybe I should do it step by step as per the standard multiplication algorithm. Let me check again. When multiplying by the tens place, you shift one position to the left, right? So when you multiply by the tens digit (5), you actually do 43*5 and then add a zero at the end. So 43*5 is 215, then adding a zero gives 2150. That matches.\n\nNow, after getting both partial products, I need to add them together. So 86 (from the ones place) plus 2150 (from the tens place). Let me add those. 2150 + 86</think>\nTherefore, the answer is "]]
